Z||000034||88CoupeV6||12-01-2001||10:53 AM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||9
Z||000035||WampusCat||12-01-2001||10:53 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by DR650SE:<BR><B>A little physics.  two cars collide at 35 mph head on with each other.  This is the effect of hitting a wall at 35 and nit 70 mph.  The two cars counter act each other and just crunh each other and act like a wall to each other.  Just thought I'd tell you this since i've taken a lil physics.  Again my thoughts out to the fam.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I'm guessing VERY little physics.  If two objects are each traveling towards the point of impact at 35mph from opposite directions, then the rate that they are closing on each other is 70mph.  That's exactly the same as if one car is parked and the other is going 70mph.  If you're going 35mph and hit a parked car, the energy involved is only 1/4 as much as going 35 and hitting that same car travelling 35mph towards you.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000036||Peachez||12-01-2001||10:58 AM||||i used to not wear my seatbelt and thought it was gay, but i will definately rethink my decision after this post.  its really sad to hear things like this if its a fiero or not.  I wonder what he was thinking right before he died?  did he know what was going to happen next?  man thats scary.  It reminds me two years ago when my best friend lost her dad in a car accident.  he didn't have his seatbelt on either.  to this day, she hasn't been the happy girl i knew her as.  I wish i could change that as well as take away all the hurt and burden that has been placed on the family and friends of victims in tradgedies such as these.  My heart goes out...||||reg||1
Z||000037||Monkeyman||12-01-2001||12:24 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by WampusCat:<BR><B> I'm guessing VERY little physics.  If two objects are each traveling towards the point of impact at 35mph from opposite directions, then the rate that they are closing on each other is 70mph.  That's exactly the same as if one car is parked and the other is going 70mph.  If you're going 35mph and hit a parked car, the energy involved is only 1/4 as much as going 35 and hitting that same car travelling 35mph towards you.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>I don't know anything about physics, but wouldn't it depend on the size of the vehicles (mass)?  Assume that everyone's going 35mph.  If a Yugo collides with a semi, wouldn't the impact (in ft lbs or however you measure it) be greater on the Yugo than the big truck?  Or would it just do more damage?  (Maybe 55mph worth of damage to the Yugo and 10mph worth of damage to the semi?)  Am I making any sense?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000038||avengador1||12-01-2001||12:26 PM||||In a rollover, most people that died were thrown from their vehicle and crushed by that vehicle rolling over them. If they had been wearing a seatbelt most would have only minor injuries. I also remember a state tropper in the 70's being quoted as saying that he never pulled a body from a wreck that was wearing a seat belt. As for the energy of my head on collision, my car was a '77 Chevy Vega wagon. The car was bent into the shape of an "L". The front bumper ended up where my knees where and the engine came right thru the firewall.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>[This message has been edited by avengador1 (edited 12-01-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by avengador1 (edited 12-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000039||Raydar||12-01-2001||12:49 PM||||A prayer for him and his family.<P>My car doesn't even hit the street if I'm not wearing a seatbelt. <BR>When I was about 21, I totaled a Chevy Monza when a Grand Prix turned across in front of me. Had a slightly skinned knee, and a stiff neck and back that lasted for a few days. That's all. Same with my GF. <BR>I usually *didn't* wear seat belts, but GF's mom used to "remind us", whenever we went anywhere. Glad we were, that day. Always have, ever since.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>From the Department of Redundancy Department.</FONT>||||reg||9
Z||000040||Mach10||12-01-2001||01:26 PM||||Heart goes out to the family. Every fatal car accident is a needless death  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P><BR>As for physics: In a crash involving 2 objects moving at the same speed towards each other, the resulting interaction (elastic/non-elastic collision) would have the same force as one of the objects hitting an immoveable object at it's own speed. YES, the apparant speed is the sum of both, but the way they both interact with each other negates this... Grade 10 physics  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||9
Z||000041||avengador1||12-01-2001||06:20 PM||||As for physics: some of the laws of motion. An object at rest tends to stay at rest, An object in motion tends to stay in motion, For every action there is an equal an opposite reaction. ||||reg||1
Z||000042||SLYGUY||12-01-2001||06:46 PM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.printroom.com/_vti_bin/ViewImage.dll?userid=SLYGUY&album_id=27041&image_id=30&courtesy=1"> <P>This car was pretty torn up. <BR>God Bless him and his family...<P>Sly||||reg||1
Z||000043||artherd||12-03-2001||03:53 AM||||Force=1/2(mass)*(Velocity)^2<P>See that ^2? that's Velocity...SQUARED.  30mph carries FOUR TIMES the kenetic energy of 15mph...   <P>If you think the two vehicles' speeds 'cancel out' then you haven't been hit while you're at a rest at a traffic light.<P>ALWAYS wear your seatbelt.<P>My heart goes out to the driver and their family.<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000044||fieroX||12-03-2001||12:11 PM||||when two identical cars hit exactly head on at identical speeds (say 35 mph) or when one of those cars hits a brick wall at 35 mph, the damage incurred on each vehicle will be the same.  The two cars hitting head on will obtain the same damage, the total damage of the accident will be split between the vehicles, and assuming the brick wall doesnt give any the damage to the other car will be equivalent.  Its as if the two cars involved in the head on crash hit an invisible brick wall at the exact point of impact.<P>My feelings are with the friends and family of the man who lost his life.http://www.fiero.nl/forum/icons/icon9.gif||||reg||1
Z||000045||FieroRacer||12-03-2001||03:35 PM||||(Bows Head for the dead Fiero)  I know, I've grown heartless when it comes to us humans, but look at the leading causes of our deaths, our own actions.  It is sad that the family will have to go through the loss though||||reg||1
Z||000046||yo_sweet||12-03-2001||10:52 PM||||Hey i bet those seats are still good  and the wing i wonder where i can find that car!<P>------------------<BR>1986 Fiero SE 2.8lv6<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/dance.gif">  <P><BR>www.geocities.com/acme_s2000/intro.html<BR>yo_sweet!||||reg||6
Z||000047||WampusCat||12-03-2001||11:07 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fieroX:<BR><B>when two identical cars hit exactly head on at identical speeds (say 35 mph) or when one of those cars hits a brick wall at 35 mph, the damage incurred on each vehicle will be the same.  The two cars hitting head on will obtain the same damage, the total damage of the accident will be split between the vehicles, and assuming the brick wall doesnt give any the damage to the other car will be equivalent.  Its as if the two cars involved in the head on crash hit an invisible brick wall at the exact point of impact.<P>My feelings are with the friends and family of the man who lost his life.http://www.fiero.nl/forum/icons/icon9.gif</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That's great for science class, but in real life there are almost no objects that you can hit that will not move or deform any at all.  Cars that hit brick walls at any apreciable speed usually go through the wall or at least break it up.  That uses up a lot of the energy.  Most cars hit other cars, and a parked car will not only move but will also geatly deform and absorb lots of energy.  The two cars will "share" the total energy of the collision and like artherd posted, that total enery is 4X as much in a head on where both cars are going 35 than when one car going 35 hits a parked car.  We're basically comparing apples and oranges here.  What you say about the theoretical immoveable object is true, what we say about the parked car is true.  sorry the guy got killed.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000048||FieroKyle||12-03-2001||11:34 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by yo_sweet:<BR><B>Hey i bet those seats are still good  and the wing i wonder where i can find that car!<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>I found that being bad humor.||||reg||13
